WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.934
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.525 --> 00:00:22.025
PRESENTER: It's a real pleasure
to welcome Bob Laughlin back

00:00:22.025 --> 00:00:23.720
TO MIT.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.240
Bob was an undergraduate
at Berkeley,

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.120
but he came to MIT to
do his graduate work.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.920
His graduate thesis was done
here with John Joannopoulos.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.950
He then moved on to Bell
Labs for a while, Lawrence

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.530
Livermore, before he finally
got a faculty position, which

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:45.080
he still has, at
Stanford University.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:50.000
Bob is best known for his theory
of the integer quantum Hall

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.410
effect, followed by his theory
of the fractional quantum Hall

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:58.820
effect, for which he shared
the Nobel Prize in 1998

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.310
with the two experimenters
who discovered

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:03.450
the fractional
quantum Hall effect,

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.540
Horst Stormer and Dan Tsui.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.980
Bob has traveled extensively.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:18.100
He has been a
spokesman for science

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.380
in a number of different areas.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:25.370
He spent two years
in South Korea

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:29.440
as the president of the
Korea Advanced Institute

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:33.190
for Science and Technology.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:36.730
He has recently
written a popular book

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:40.180
on the philosophy
of science, a book

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.300
called A Different Universe.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.360
Not an alternate universe,
a different universe,

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:51.340
with the subtitle "Reinventing
Physics from the Bottom Down."

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.070
Now, I know that that's a
well read book because just

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:58.690
in the break, someone came up to
me spontaneously and mentioned

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:02.020
that Bob, he had read your book
and he enjoyed it very much,

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.320
so he was looking forward to
what you were going to tell us.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.160
Bob.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.560
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.710
Good morning, everybody.

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:20.350
Obviously, it's a
great pleasure to be

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:26.290
back at MIT, a place where
things change, but also many

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:30.580
do not, such as
numbering the buildings.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:31.564
[LAUGHS]

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:36.180
However, that's good
because then you

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.420
know right where you stand.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:47.370
Now, when I got asked
to speak for this event,

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.550
I did a little thinking
about the topic,

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:53.402
because it's rather important.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:05.790
I tried to imagine what was
the intent of the people who

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.520
lobbied to get the money,
the people who donated

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:15.690
the funds to build a center,
and more generally, what

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:20.500
the vision of the center is,
in the context of physics

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.100
with a small p.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:29.230
And the title I came up with
was Renaissance Physicist.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:32.470
That is to say, physicists
who know a lot of things.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:40.740
So let's talk about
that a little bit,

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:44.700
because that's pretty clearly
what the center is all about.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.770
We talk about bringing
together, and so forth.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.310
Why would you want
to do that, however?

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:55.850
What's to be gained
by bringing together?

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:59.210
After all, you've
work pretty well apart

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:02.000
for a long, long time.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.820
And there's an old
saying about machines,

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:08.190
of course, if it ain't
broke, don't fix it.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:11.930
So the question you can ask
is, well, what are you fixing?

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:18.040
And what is the objective,
really, of the center?

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:22.000
And I must alert everybody that
this talk actually, by design,

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.460
is directed at the younger
people more than the old thuds.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.530
Because the problem
I'm going to talk about

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:33.310
is your problem, not the problem
of your academic forebears.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:41.650
Now, I will take off my MIT
hat and put on my Stanford hat

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:45.160
and tell you that MIT is not
the only organization that

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:48.850
is worried sick
about this problem.

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:51.820
Stanford also has
gone to great lengths

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:57.540
to try to bring the branches of
physics together, in some ways

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.540
more successfully than others.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:08.320
My informant of physics,
Professor C. N. Herring,

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:14.590
who is now not there
completely, felt the same way,

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:21.520
that we are trying to invent
physics with a small p.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.190
That's what we're trying
to do as intellectuals,

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.560
and we're always fighting
the centrifugal forces

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.530
that try to make it not happen.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:36.370
He didn't succeed.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:38.460
And I think it's fair to
say that a lot of people

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:46.600
who have omnivorous interests
haven't succeeded, either.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:51.920
The reason, of course,
is the funding algorithm

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:55.910
with which we interact
with government, and also

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:56.855
historical patterns.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:03.310
The reason we need to think
hard about this problem

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:07.690
is, of course, that the
historical milieu was changing.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:11.200
That's exactly what
Shirley was talking

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:14.020
about when she mentioned
the rather dark clouds

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:15.235
on the horizon for science.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:21.660
The division of labor in
science and technology

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:26.640
that we're familiar with is,
in fact, a Cold War legacy.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.100
And like many things
that our Cold War

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:37.260
legacy is, it's going away,
in part, at least I think so.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:40.650
In other words, that
the physics departments

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:49.590
that don't create physics
with a small p won't survive.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:53.190
So it's very important.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:56.970
Now, we might ask the
question, if there

00:06:56.970 --> 00:07:00.720
had been no World War II
and no Cold War, why would

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.080
we have physics departments?

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.220
After all, the
science that really

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.610
makes the heart of
industrial people beat

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:19.110
is chemistry and, of course, its
doppelganger, material science.

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.240
Material sciences and
engineering departments now

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.510
to basically, it
solves the chemistry.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.760
There's a lot that
happens in materials

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:28.980
and in organic
chemistry that are kind

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:32.370
of magical and unpredictable,
and you can make lots of money

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:33.360
selling them.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.940
And so our industrial
friends are mainly concerned

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:39.510
about that.

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:47.900
Also, engineering,
electrical engineering,

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:49.530
mechanical engineering,
in particular,

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:51.640
continue to be
strong disciplines,

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:54.400
despite the heavy
challenges that are

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:55.960
coming to us from the Far East.

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:01.350
Well, there are two
reasons, I think.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.990
And of course, this is
something that all of us who

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:06.060
are in physics have
to think through,

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.520
but I'm going to give
you my personal opinion.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.670
It has to do with the topic.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:18.620
In a technical milieu,
which is where we are,

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:22.340
some of the people
have to know things.

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:25.250
Some of the technical
people on your staff

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:31.640
have to just have a wild,
childlike curiosity,

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:37.750
where they demand to understand
everything, everything.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:41.120
In other words, they
need some physicists.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:47.270
Now, I'm saying this
from long experience.

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:49.340
There's something about
the physics discipline

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:54.380
that manages to capture
the most arrogant people

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:56.760
in the university.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:57.380
Yes.

00:08:57.380 --> 00:08:58.342
[LAUGHS]

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:03.960
Not surprisingly, such
people are not always

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:07.410
welcome inside
engineering houses.

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:09.330
And you're laughing, but
it's absolutely true.

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:11.970
It turns out we're a
very dangerous people.

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:15.750
In industrial environment, it's
the physicists who walk out

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:17.880
the door with your technology.

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:20.588
They understand it, and
they walk out the door

00:09:20.588 --> 00:09:21.630
and they start a company.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:23.922
And of course, that's very
good for starting companies,

00:09:23.922 --> 00:09:29.810
but very bad for defending
your own company against.

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:31.550
That's intellectual
property theft.

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:44.990
So the industrial value
of an omnivorous person

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:47.450
is actually
problematical, and has

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:52.910
been from the very beginning,
actually, at least I think so.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:54.950
And that's my
experience, having worked

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:57.650
in industrial
environments myself.

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:00.080
This is true, by the way,
even inside nuclear weapons

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:01.130
laboratories.

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:04.957
People who know lots of
things are really not welcome

00:10:04.957 --> 00:10:06.290
because they're dangerous there.

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:11.300
In that case, they're
dangerous to national security.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:16.380
No, our value, especially
in engineering school,

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:19.780
is actually to parents.

00:10:19.780 --> 00:10:22.270
Now, people who run
universities, of course,

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:27.910
like to hear that, because
the money, the cash cow

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:31.420
part of our business, is
undergraduate education.

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:32.725
But it's actually true.

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:41.900
There's a joke in
physics, in science,

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:45.600
you garner power by telling
people what you know,

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:48.090
whereas in engineering,
you garner people

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:50.900
by preventing them from
knowing what you know.

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:52.600
This is, of course, not a joke.

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:54.120
It's actually true.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.078
Very good engineers
know a lot of things

00:10:56.078 --> 00:10:57.120
that they don't tell you.

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:04.220
Unfortunately, that
behavior is fundamentally

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:09.270
incompatible with the
entire concept of education.

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:13.460
So in an engineering
school like MIT,

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:16.290
and other schools that have
engineering sides to them,

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:19.100
you have a fundamental conflict
because between the needs

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:21.320
of the industrial
buyers, on the one hand,

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:24.060
and the needs of the
parents on the other.

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:26.630
And those of us who are
in the knowledge business

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:30.920
are actually serving the causes
of education, which is to say,

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:34.980
the causes of parents.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:40.850
This is the reason why you can't
close the physics department.

00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:42.860
A lot of universities
are doing just

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:45.810
that, they're closing
their physics departments.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:47.630
But an engineering
school, like MIT,

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:49.610
cannot close its
engineering departments,

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:57.750
because we are the Renaissance
people of the technical world.

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:01.370
It is a physicist who will
tell you clearly and tersely

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:05.417
how a thing works, and
probably only a physicist.

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:15.940
Thus, our future is
tied with clarity,

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:24.120
with omnivorous interests, with
understanding things that are

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:27.930
other people's quasi-property.

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:32.610
Basically, being an
arrogant know-it-all.

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:36.720
At least I think so,
and that's the way

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:39.820
I've been pushing my own
departments at Stanford.

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:45.390
And I think that's the
underlying impetus of the Green

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:47.290
Center.

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:51.100
The younger people who will
fill it have a job to do,

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:54.910
and their job is to
create intellectualism

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:57.100
an industrial environment
that is fundamentally

00:12:57.100 --> 00:13:02.068
hostile to intellectualism.

00:13:02.068 --> 00:13:06.980
Now, I want to talk a little
bit more about that hostility,

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:11.690
because it's very important
to the task ahead,

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:13.457
both for the
physics departments,

00:13:13.457 --> 00:13:14.915
generally, and for
younger faculty.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:21.330
Tom mentioned I'd
written a book.

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:22.440
You know, I'm a physicist.

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:25.050
I'm one of the arrogant people
I've been talking about.

00:13:25.050 --> 00:13:29.873
I have many hats, and one of
them turns out to be literary.

00:13:29.873 --> 00:13:32.040
You know, there's that old
story, you've got lemons,

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:32.790
you make lemonade.

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:38.050
Well, I found I could write
and it makes money, so hey.

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:41.612
Well, I have a new
product coming out.

00:13:41.612 --> 00:13:43.070
We're in contract
in New York now--

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:44.820
it's actually coming
out in Germany first,

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:46.770
but this is a mere detail--

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:50.460
called Crime of Reason.

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:54.270
And this new book is
very, very relevant

00:13:54.270 --> 00:14:01.370
to the thing we're discussing
today, which is knowing things.

00:14:01.370 --> 00:14:02.540
The book is very dark.

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:08.810
The premise is that the
sequestration of knowledge

00:14:08.810 --> 00:14:14.060
that has happened during my
lifetime has become so severe

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:18.680
and has accelerated so
much that it's actually now

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:23.555
against the law to figure out
certain things for yourself.

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:31.720
Now, physicists normally don't
think about that too much,

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:33.700
because you know, we're
very arrogant people.

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:36.550
We think we have a right
to understand everything.

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:38.720
But if you think
about it a minute,

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:40.810
there are some kinds
of physics that

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:42.907
are obviously very,
very important

00:14:42.907 --> 00:14:43.990
that you never talk about.

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:49.800
You don't think about
the computer codes

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:54.360
to calculate that
thing don't exist.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:57.630
Well, when you get inside the
fence of a weapons laboratory,

00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:00.000
you'll understand why.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.060
Those particular
things were identified

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:07.140
as highly dangerous
knowledge in 1954,

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:10.680
and expressly banned
from the public domain

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:14.610
by the Atomic
Energy Act of 1954.

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:17.160
Now, I can't tell you
what those things are,

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:19.170
because telling
you what's banned

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:22.853
is itself a violation of the
Atomic Energy Act of 1954.

00:15:22.853 --> 00:15:24.270
But you're very,
very smart people

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:25.920
so you can figure
it out for yourself.

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:28.040
Just ask yourself,
where are the holes?

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:30.030
OK, where are the things
you don't talk about,

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:33.240
and there you are.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:38.560
Now, it is part of the
cultural transition that

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:42.040
happened when the nuclear
weapon was invented,

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:43.560
that our society--

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:44.830
well, let's say generally.

00:15:44.830 --> 00:15:47.730
Western society,
generally, decided

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:50.440
that there were certain
kinds of scientific knowledge

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:56.110
that were simply too
dangerous to be widely known

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:58.180
and you had to get rid of them.

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:00.700
And you had to prevent
people, financially,

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:03.220
from figuring them out.

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:05.590
You had to cut off their grants.

00:16:05.590 --> 00:16:08.950
Basically, stop research
in those particular areas.

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:14.370
Now, who decides where
those places are?

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:17.670
Well, of course,
that's the problem.

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:21.890
In the case of
nuclear technology,

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:25.740
the really dangerous things
that were banned, of course,

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:28.650
were well identified at the
time because the technology

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:29.460
was already mature.

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:36.760
But other things
that came later,

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:39.490
it's less clear, less clear.

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:42.290
There are big hunks of
semiconductor technology

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:46.100
which are completely off limits.

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:49.840
There are big hunks
of laser technology

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:51.320
that are completely off limits.

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:53.700
Now, these are not banned
by the Atomic Energy Act,

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:57.980
so I'm not violating any
laws by talking about them.

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:02.260
So that means that in physics,
as a practical matter, the idea

00:17:02.260 --> 00:17:05.800
of physicists, people
who know everything,

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:10.060
is in conflict,
fundamentally, not only

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:16.260
with trends historically,
but the law, the law.

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:20.569
It's actually against the
law to think about things.

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:21.869
There is a famous case.

00:17:21.869 --> 00:17:24.248
There's a number of them.

00:17:24.248 --> 00:17:27.420
Probably the most famous is
the Morland hydrogen bomb case,

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:33.650
where a journalist named
Howard Morland published

00:17:33.650 --> 00:17:39.190
a piece about guesses about
how hydrogen bonds work

00:17:39.190 --> 00:17:44.140
that the FBI stopped,
or attempted to stop,

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:47.320
but actually didn't
succeed in doing so.

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:51.160
And the judge in the case
basically affirmed the idea

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:56.170
that the safety of the
public against certain kinds

00:17:56.170 --> 00:18:01.740
of technology overrode,
manifestly overrode,

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:04.260
the right to speech,
and moreover, the right

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:05.640
to even think about it.

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:11.820
Now, you might ask, isn't
that a civil rights issue?

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:13.590
And indeed, it is.

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:15.770
It's a civil rights issue
that has never played out

00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:19.980
in the court for a reason.

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:22.910
You can guess, let's say,
from the Wen Ho Lee case

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:28.700
that those kinds of things
might, if brought to court,

00:18:28.700 --> 00:18:31.430
strike down the
Atomic Energy Act,

00:18:31.430 --> 00:18:34.100
and the people whose job
it is to protect security

00:18:34.100 --> 00:18:36.060
really don't want that.

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:41.030
So as a practical matter,
what's happened, let's say,

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:45.350
since 1950 is that the
potential confrontations

00:18:45.350 --> 00:18:47.330
of intellectual
freedom on the one hand

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:52.700
and national security on
the other have been avoided.

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:55.050
Attorney General
John Ashcroft made

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:57.460
a statement, quite amazing.

00:18:57.460 --> 00:18:59.990
He said that in the
entire 50 years,

00:18:59.990 --> 00:19:02.160
the criminal provisions
of the Atomic Energy Act

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:06.630
had only been prosecuted
in court twice.

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:10.500
Now, let's say the
non-espionage ones,

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:14.610
and let us guess that it
is not feasible that there

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:16.810
were only two violations.

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:20.640
So it's not too hard to guess
that the atomic energy probably

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:24.670
is in fundamental conflict
with the first Amendment.

00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:30.720
Now, that's a matter that's
played out in physics,

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:33.630
but of course, not completely
because it's repeating itself

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:36.180
now in biology.

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:38.760
And those of you who
have biology interests

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:42.840
know that there is
disappearing knowledge

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:46.710
in the case of biology also
right now, although it's not

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:48.240
forbidden by statute.

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:49.980
It's actually
disappearing by virtue

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:51.285
of the funding algorithm.

00:19:51.285 --> 00:19:56.670
It's just you can't get
money to do certain things.

00:19:56.670 --> 00:20:00.480
Word on the street is it's very
hard to get bacteria money.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.910
So the people who pay
for biological research

00:20:03.910 --> 00:20:07.090
are happy for you to work
on eukaryotes, not so

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:11.320
happy to work on organisms
that might be highly, highly

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:12.010
dangerous.

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:21.360
Now, there's also the little
matter of private property,

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:27.510
and as you know, we've
had madly accelerating--

00:20:27.510 --> 00:20:30.620
well, let's say
strengthening-- strengthening

00:20:30.620 --> 00:20:34.310
of intellectual property
laws in the past 15 years,

00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:40.430
basically, as defense against
IP transfer across the Pacific.

00:20:40.430 --> 00:20:46.070
And that means that
the most amazing things

00:20:46.070 --> 00:20:49.040
are becoming patented.

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:52.910
For example, Microsoft
has patented the verb.

00:20:52.910 --> 00:20:54.620
I'm not making this up.

00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:56.255
And Cingular has
patented the smiley.

00:20:59.190 --> 00:21:04.620
Amazon.com has patented the
entire idea of the checkout

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:05.835
basket on your computer.

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:14.180
Could we patent Maxwell's
equations today?

00:21:14.180 --> 00:21:16.360
I think so.

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:17.177
I think you could.

00:21:17.177 --> 00:21:18.260
You could make it illegal.

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:20.650
You can make it against the
basic copyright violation

00:21:20.650 --> 00:21:22.930
to have that shirt right there.

00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:23.430
Right?

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:24.020
[LAUGHS]

00:21:24.495 --> 00:21:25.870
It doesn't matter
if God said it.

00:21:25.870 --> 00:21:27.040
The Supreme Court says no.

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:36.140
Well, so is this
historical trend

00:21:36.140 --> 00:21:39.530
correlated with the stress we've
all been feeling in physics,

00:21:39.530 --> 00:21:41.960
as it gets harder and
harder to raise money

00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:43.820
to make things clear?

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:45.320
OK.

00:21:45.320 --> 00:21:47.870
Well, that's a matter of debate,
but my own personal opinion

00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:49.070
is yes.

00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:53.250
I think they are different
sides of the same coin,

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:56.750
and that the era that we're
in right now, and still in,

00:21:56.750 --> 00:22:01.240
is an era of darkness,
in which the entire idea

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:08.110
of public domain knowledge is
being rejected by people very

00:22:08.110 --> 00:22:11.500
high up in our society, and
implemented in the laws.

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:20.650
Now, what will
happen in the future?

00:22:20.650 --> 00:22:24.610
Of course, it hasn't
played out yet.

00:22:24.610 --> 00:22:28.310
And I guess that's kind
of what's on my mind here.

00:22:28.310 --> 00:22:33.870
I think of myself
as a physicist,

00:22:33.870 --> 00:22:38.600
I think of myself as a
rather revolutionary person.

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:40.970
Of course, the
physicists that we revere

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:46.190
are all fairly
revolutionary people,

00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:49.400
but revolutionary in
the sense of something

00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:52.760
a little deeper and more
important and more global.

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:57.880
And that is physics
departments are the place,

00:22:57.880 --> 00:23:02.290
in a technical university,
where the entire Western concept

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:05.800
of the light of
knowledge is safe,

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:12.260
because physicists cannot live
without understanding things.

00:23:12.260 --> 00:23:15.080
It is hateful to
a physicist to let

00:23:15.080 --> 00:23:18.260
something remain ununderstood.

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:20.330
And that means
that physicists are

00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:24.110
tailor-made to be
trouble makers when

00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:27.930
it comes to the
sequestration of knowledge.

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:31.890
Now, whether that will make
our society safer and more

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:35.740
healthy in the long run or
more dangerous in the long run,

00:23:35.740 --> 00:23:38.680
I don't know.

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:43.960
But I do know that
that's the mission.

00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:46.180
As the cloud of
darkness descends

00:23:46.180 --> 00:23:50.650
over technical
knowledge in the world,

00:23:50.650 --> 00:23:54.100
it's our job, as a
discipline of science

00:23:54.100 --> 00:23:58.120
and a discipline of
engineering, also,

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:00.610
to make sure it doesn't go out.

00:24:03.410 --> 00:24:07.600
In other words, our
job is to make sure

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:13.500
that the concept of reason,
the science of reason,

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:14.840
does not perish from the earth.

00:24:17.780 --> 00:24:21.740
So you younger people who
are populating that center up

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:25.330
there, I'm sure you'll
mostly go around

00:24:25.330 --> 00:24:31.180
about your business as usual,
because that's what they do.

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:36.070
But please, please keep in
mind that the clock is ticking.

00:24:36.070 --> 00:24:40.350
We have a number of
more difficult years

00:24:40.350 --> 00:24:43.980
to go through, in my
opinion, and there

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:48.450
will be incredible pressures
on people to specialize and not

00:24:48.450 --> 00:24:51.570
explain things to each other.

00:24:51.570 --> 00:25:01.020
Because that's what people
afraid of knowledge want.

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:02.960
That's how you manage
a corporation, how

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:04.730
you keep your
engineering safe, is

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:08.930
you make sure people
don't talk to each other.

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:16.210
So I think what you have to
do is create something new,

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:20.530
something that everybody else in
technical life and universities

00:25:20.530 --> 00:25:23.680
is trying to create also,
but unfortunately, we

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:28.910
don't have a nice, big
center in which to do it.

00:25:28.910 --> 00:25:34.640
Therefore, the donors
and the management team

00:25:34.640 --> 00:25:39.190
of the university have
given you the tool

00:25:39.190 --> 00:25:45.410
to do something really great
and important historically.

00:25:45.410 --> 00:25:47.915
The rest is up to you.

00:25:47.915 --> 00:25:49.400
Godspeed.

00:25:49.400 --> 00:25:52.370
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:05.470 --> 00:26:08.440
PRESENTER: So our next speaker,
Steve Weinberg, I think,

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:12.040
is truly a
Renaissance physicist.

00:26:12.040 --> 00:26:14.440
He is the Josey
Regental professor

00:26:14.440 --> 00:26:17.560
of physics at the
University of Texas Austin,

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:19.690
and his imprint can
be found everywhere

00:26:19.690 --> 00:26:23.590
in modern and reductivist
physics, in particle physics,

00:26:23.590 --> 00:26:27.700
in astrophysics,
and in cosmology.

00:26:27.700 --> 00:26:30.010
More and more, as
time goes on, he

00:26:30.010 --> 00:26:35.080
has emerged as a spokesman
for secular human values

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:36.970
and high culture
in a world that's

00:26:36.970 --> 00:26:40.030
been swept by darker forces.

00:26:40.030 --> 00:26:42.520
Steve was a visiting
professor here,

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:48.670
and then professor of physics
at MIT from 1967 to 1973.

00:26:48.670 --> 00:26:52.330
This was a period of profound
change in elementary particle

00:26:52.330 --> 00:26:57.700
physics, and he played a central
role, and in many aspects,

00:26:57.700 --> 00:27:00.970
the central role
in that revolution.

00:27:00.970 --> 00:27:04.540
We'd like to think that on
one of the slate blackboards

00:27:04.540 --> 00:27:06.850
still hanging in
the CTP, perhaps

00:27:06.850 --> 00:27:11.530
in some graduate student's
office now, in 1967,

00:27:11.530 --> 00:27:13.438
Steve first wrote down
the equations that

00:27:13.438 --> 00:27:15.730
have come to be known as the
standard model of particle

00:27:15.730 --> 00:27:17.140
physics.

00:27:17.140 --> 00:27:20.332
We often invoke this idea
when the construction team

00:27:20.332 --> 00:27:22.540
was threatening to throw
away some of the blackboard,

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:25.025
because we didn't
know which one it was.

00:27:25.025 --> 00:27:25.900
We made sure of that.

00:27:25.900 --> 00:27:26.540
[LAUGHS]

00:27:26.540 --> 00:27:28.930
And therefore, they all had
to be preserved and reused

00:27:28.930 --> 00:27:30.355
in student and faculty offices.

00:27:33.850 --> 00:27:36.985
Steve's paper in 1967,
"A Model of Hadrons,"

00:27:36.985 --> 00:27:40.300
marks the birth of--

00:27:40.300 --> 00:27:40.810
excuse me.

00:27:40.810 --> 00:27:43.930
It says leptons there,
too, "Model of Leptons."

00:27:43.930 --> 00:27:46.900
If only with then we
had a model of hadrons--

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:51.010
marks the birth of the modern
era in particle physics,

00:27:51.010 --> 00:27:53.170
and was the first
unification of forces

00:27:53.170 --> 00:27:55.810
since Faraday and Maxwell
unified electricity

00:27:55.810 --> 00:27:58.330
and magnetism in
the 19th century.

00:27:58.330 --> 00:28:01.480
It led to a Nobel Prize that
he shared with Abdus Salam

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:05.920
and Shelley Glashow in 1979.

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:08.590
Last week, I was at
a meeting concerning

00:28:08.590 --> 00:28:12.340
Islamic physics, in which
Abdus Salam was mentioned.

00:28:12.340 --> 00:28:16.180
He is, without doubt, the
leading Pakistani physicist

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:17.180
in history.

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:19.660
He is also not
honored in his country

00:28:19.660 --> 00:28:21.580
because he was a
member of a minority

00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:25.030
sect in the Muslim world.

00:28:25.030 --> 00:28:27.640
And when he was being
discussed, the person

00:28:27.640 --> 00:28:29.290
who was talking about
him said that he

00:28:29.290 --> 00:28:33.250
was the recipient of one of
the really big Nobel prizes

00:28:33.250 --> 00:28:34.736
of the 20th century.

00:28:34.736 --> 00:28:35.668
[LAUGHS]

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:40.330
Steve's contributions
to physics are really

00:28:40.330 --> 00:28:42.020
far too numerous
to mention here,

00:28:42.020 --> 00:28:45.280
so I'm just going to
mention one is an icon.

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:48.820
In 1988, he wrote
a paper, in which

00:28:48.820 --> 00:28:52.300
he made a prediction of the dark
energy density in the universe

00:28:52.300 --> 00:28:55.690
based on anthropic arguments.

00:28:55.690 --> 00:28:58.930
In recent years, observations of
the cosmic microwave background

00:28:58.930 --> 00:29:02.890
radiation have proved
that prediction correct.

00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:08.420
70% of the universe is in this
mysterious form of dark energy.

00:29:08.420 --> 00:29:12.100
Now, this is, as far as I know,
the only successful prediction

00:29:12.100 --> 00:29:15.040
that's been made on the
basis of anthropic logic,

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:18.100
but also, it ranks
second in scope only

00:29:18.100 --> 00:29:21.980
to Alan Guth in the
scope of his predictions.

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:25.700
He predicted the identity of 70%
of the energy in the universe,

00:29:25.700 --> 00:29:27.640
and of course, Alan,
through inflation,

00:29:27.640 --> 00:29:30.690
predicted the total
energy in the universe.

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:32.595
So pretty good, Steve.

00:29:32.595 --> 00:29:33.505
[LAUGHS]

00:29:37.048 --> 00:29:38.590
More recently, Steve
has been playing

00:29:38.590 --> 00:29:40.180
in the big leagues
of culture wars

00:29:40.180 --> 00:29:42.700
that have swept the
academic and political world

00:29:42.700 --> 00:29:44.380
over the past years.

00:29:44.380 --> 00:29:48.130
In fact, if you go online,
you can read of many books,

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:51.070
and you can read many
book reviews, interviews,

00:29:51.070 --> 00:29:53.830
and opinion pieces
that Steve has written,

00:29:53.830 --> 00:29:58.900
and they are wonderful fonts of
both wisdom and human sympathy.

00:29:58.900 --> 00:30:00.790
I'll just quote
briefly from one.

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:04.000
Steve was reviewing a book that
had been written several years

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:07.840
ago by Stephen Wolfram,
introducing a new paradigm

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:09.980
for modern science.

00:30:09.980 --> 00:30:13.510
It's about 700
pages and 20 pounds.

00:30:13.510 --> 00:30:19.090
And Steve wrote quite
a restrained review,

00:30:19.090 --> 00:30:21.160
a polite and restrained review.

00:30:21.160 --> 00:30:25.650
At the end, Wolfram
was advocating--

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:27.875
Wolfram's a computer
scientist-- and Wolfram

00:30:27.875 --> 00:30:29.500
was advocating that
the universe should

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:31.450
be viewed as a giant computer.

00:30:31.450 --> 00:30:35.920
And Steve closes his review by
saying, so might a carpenter,

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:40.900
looking at the moon, suppose
that it is made of wood?

00:30:40.900 --> 00:30:45.030
And so with that,
let me introduce

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:46.431
Steve Weinberg to you.

00:30:46.431 --> 00:30:48.836
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:55.120 --> 00:30:57.730
Thank you very much, Bob.

00:30:57.730 --> 00:31:01.270
As others have said, it's
wonderful to be back.

00:31:01.270 --> 00:31:03.430
Thanks for having me here.

00:31:03.430 --> 00:31:10.390
In thinking over
this talk, I was

00:31:10.390 --> 00:31:13.960
flooded by happy
memories of my time here.

00:31:13.960 --> 00:31:18.910
And so I'm going to burden
you, in at least the first part

00:31:18.910 --> 00:31:22.690
of my talk, with a number
of personal reminiscences,

00:31:22.690 --> 00:31:25.300
and at the same time
keep you from your lunch.

00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:37.080
A century ago, when I decided to
become a theoretical physicist,

00:31:37.080 --> 00:31:43.110
I felt, to some extent,
that I was taking the veil.

00:31:43.110 --> 00:31:45.600
I loved physics, but
at the same time,

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:48.270
I was attracted
to public affairs,

00:31:48.270 --> 00:31:52.540
with the opportunity to do
some good in the real world.

00:31:52.540 --> 00:31:55.520
And I regretted giving that up.

00:31:55.520 --> 00:31:58.430
I knew, of course, of
physicists who had been

00:31:58.430 --> 00:32:01.280
very active in the real world.

00:32:01.280 --> 00:32:04.820
In World War II,
Oppenheimer and Robbie,

00:32:04.820 --> 00:32:07.160
for example, in
the United States,

00:32:07.160 --> 00:32:10.820
Lindemann and RV
Jones in Britain.

00:32:10.820 --> 00:32:15.290
But it seemed to me that those
who had remained influential

00:32:15.290 --> 00:32:20.620
after the war did so at some
cost to their own research.

00:32:20.620 --> 00:32:23.070
I didn't want to pay that price.

00:32:23.070 --> 00:32:25.910
And I didn't think I'd
be very good, anyway,

00:32:25.910 --> 00:32:30.440
as an insider dealing
with government officials.

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:36.290
At MIT, I learned that
there was a different mode

00:32:36.290 --> 00:32:39.980
of being active
in public affairs,

00:32:39.980 --> 00:32:43.970
that it was possible to
work as an outsider giving

00:32:43.970 --> 00:32:49.370
technical advice to the public,
and in some cases to Congress,

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:53.810
rather than as an insider
advising government officials.

00:32:56.710 --> 00:32:59.810
I'm going to mention two
examples of this, both of them

00:32:59.810 --> 00:33:02.480
centered here at MIT.

00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:06.590
The first is the
missile defense debate.

00:33:06.590 --> 00:33:12.110
In March 1979, President
Nixon announced

00:33:12.110 --> 00:33:18.470
that the US was going to deploy
a massive new antiballistic

00:33:18.470 --> 00:33:20.840
missile system
called the Safeguard

00:33:20.840 --> 00:33:26.420
system at 12 sites in America,
which would have given,

00:33:26.420 --> 00:33:28.640
at least nominally,
complete coverage

00:33:28.640 --> 00:33:32.390
of the continental
United States.

00:33:32.390 --> 00:33:36.110
Senator Edward Kennedy
asked Jerry Wiesner,

00:33:36.110 --> 00:33:41.720
who was then provost of MIT, and
Abram Chayes from the local law

00:33:41.720 --> 00:33:47.630
school up the river,
to prepare a book that

00:33:47.630 --> 00:33:50.780
would provide technical
advice for the public

00:33:50.780 --> 00:33:57.980
and for Congress assessing this
proposal, judging its wisdom.

00:33:57.980 --> 00:34:04.220
And Jerry Wiesner asked me
to help, in a small way,

00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:10.040
with preparing the book,
along with A. Chayes.

00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:12.020
I jumped at the chance.

00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:14.570
I had done a little
work on missile defense

00:34:14.570 --> 00:34:17.929
as a member of the JASON
group of consultants.

00:34:17.929 --> 00:34:19.760
And like many
other people, I had

00:34:19.760 --> 00:34:22.880
come to feel that this
was a terrible idea,

00:34:22.880 --> 00:34:28.760
both because it would be largely
ineffective, and to the extent

00:34:28.760 --> 00:34:31.670
that it seemed effective
to the Soviets,

00:34:31.670 --> 00:34:34.850
it would only lead them to
increase the number of missiles

00:34:34.850 --> 00:34:41.120
aimed at us and/or to increase
their likeliness to use them

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:41.735
in a crisis.

00:34:45.230 --> 00:34:47.780
We asked a number
of other people

00:34:47.780 --> 00:34:52.730
to contribute to
this, some at MIT,

00:34:52.730 --> 00:34:56.090
Bernie Feld, JCR Licklider--

00:34:56.090 --> 00:34:58.430
I never found out what
those initials were for--

00:34:58.430 --> 00:35:01.010
and George Rathjens.

00:35:01.010 --> 00:35:06.320
And it's characteristic
of MIT that the boundaries

00:35:06.320 --> 00:35:08.480
between different
departments, it seems to me,

00:35:08.480 --> 00:35:11.960
are more permeable here
than in any other university

00:35:11.960 --> 00:35:12.680
I've been at.

00:35:15.410 --> 00:35:19.010
Wiesner was, of course,
an electrical engineer.

00:35:19.010 --> 00:35:21.785
I think Licklider was probably
and electrical engineering,

00:35:21.785 --> 00:35:24.680
and these days, he would
be in computer sciences.

00:35:24.680 --> 00:35:26.600
Bernie Feld was in the
physics department.

00:35:26.600 --> 00:35:28.820
George Rathjens
was in the Center

00:35:28.820 --> 00:35:30.950
for International Studies.

00:35:30.950 --> 00:35:34.280
And we also got some
support from people

00:35:34.280 --> 00:35:41.000
outside MIT, Hans Bethe,
Carl Kazen, Bill Moyers,

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:43.250
and Arthur Goldberg.

00:35:43.250 --> 00:35:47.150
In the end, the Safeguard
system was not deployed.

00:35:47.150 --> 00:35:50.480
I don't think it was very
much because of our book.

00:35:50.480 --> 00:35:53.840
By the way, our book came out
with a really snappy title.

00:35:53.840 --> 00:35:57.320
It was ABM, An Evaluation
of the Decision

00:35:57.320 --> 00:36:00.470
to Deploy an Antiballistic
Missile System,

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:03.500
and then it was translated
into German with the title

00:36:03.500 --> 00:36:04.355
Rakete Krieg.

00:36:04.355 --> 00:36:05.500
[LAUGHS]

00:36:07.280 --> 00:36:08.786
Rocket Wars.

00:36:12.190 --> 00:36:14.810
While the system
was not deployed,

00:36:14.810 --> 00:36:16.490
I don't think it was
because of our book

00:36:16.490 --> 00:36:19.410
but because, to the
surprise of everyone,

00:36:19.410 --> 00:36:23.120
both pro- and anti-ABM.

00:36:23.120 --> 00:36:26.360
People who lived in
the suburbs in America,

00:36:26.360 --> 00:36:29.360
where the missile sites
were going to be located,

00:36:29.360 --> 00:36:32.540
decided they didn't want
anti-missile missiles

00:36:32.540 --> 00:36:33.890
in their neighborhood.

00:36:33.890 --> 00:36:35.960
And that killed it
much more effectively

00:36:35.960 --> 00:36:40.640
than any learned arguments
that we were able to provide.

00:36:40.640 --> 00:36:46.100
The other example, it came
out of the student unrest

00:36:46.100 --> 00:36:50.930
in the 1968-69 academic year.

00:36:50.930 --> 00:36:56.030
Partly as a response to that
unrest, in December 1968,

00:36:56.030 --> 00:37:00.260
about 50 faculty
members, including

00:37:00.260 --> 00:37:02.130
from the physics department--

00:37:02.130 --> 00:37:07.160
Viki Weisskopf, Herman Feshbach,
Francis Lowe, Kurt Gottfried,

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:09.860
and me--

00:37:09.860 --> 00:37:11.150
prepared a statement.

00:37:14.210 --> 00:37:18.680
And we called for, and I quote,
"Scientists and engineers

00:37:18.680 --> 00:37:21.380
at MIT, and throughout
the country,

00:37:21.380 --> 00:37:24.860
to unite for concerted
action and leadership,

00:37:24.860 --> 00:37:27.890
to initiate a critical
and continued examination

00:37:27.890 --> 00:37:31.040
of governmental
policy in areas where

00:37:31.040 --> 00:37:35.120
science and technology
are of actual or potential

00:37:35.120 --> 00:37:37.790
significance."

00:37:37.790 --> 00:37:40.640
And in a way that I don't
think would have been possible

00:37:40.640 --> 00:37:43.310
at any other university,
this actually

00:37:43.310 --> 00:37:45.950
crystallized into
an organization,

00:37:45.950 --> 00:37:48.560
the Union of
Concerned Scientists,

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:50.840
which survives to the present.

00:37:50.840 --> 00:37:53.520
Herman Feshbach and
Francis Lowe were briefly

00:37:53.520 --> 00:37:56.810
the first and second chairmen,
and then Henry Kendall

00:37:56.810 --> 00:37:58.910
took over for a number of years.

00:37:58.910 --> 00:38:01.490
Henry's main interest,
at least at first,

00:38:01.490 --> 00:38:04.560
was in the dangers
of nuclear power,

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:08.650
but the scope of the USC--

00:38:08.650 --> 00:38:13.770
I'll use that abbreviation--
expanded to include

00:38:13.770 --> 00:38:17.390
the national defense
policy, science policy,

00:38:17.390 --> 00:38:23.570
and it expanded further under
Kurt Gottfried's chairmanship--

00:38:23.570 --> 00:38:26.750
he became chairman in 1999--

00:38:26.750 --> 00:38:31.100
and includes things
like just the issue

00:38:31.100 --> 00:38:34.610
that was raised in a
question to Shirley

00:38:34.610 --> 00:38:41.360
Jackson, the issue of the
response of government

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:42.410
to the voice of science.

00:38:45.380 --> 00:38:49.890
The Union of Concerned
Scientists is highly effective.

00:38:49.890 --> 00:38:53.300
I think it is probably
the most effective voice

00:38:53.300 --> 00:38:57.230
of science on public
issues in America,

00:38:57.230 --> 00:39:00.480
although there are others.

00:39:00.480 --> 00:39:06.410
Its statements, its
reports, are competent.

00:39:06.410 --> 00:39:09.170
It only comments
on issues that are

00:39:09.170 --> 00:39:11.540
within its scope of expertise.

00:39:11.540 --> 00:39:13.850
So it doesn't make statements
about the war in Iraq,

00:39:13.850 --> 00:39:15.890
or things like that.

00:39:15.890 --> 00:39:18.140
Their statements are honest.

00:39:18.140 --> 00:39:24.560
It doesn't shade its conclusions
for a pre-determined aim,

00:39:24.560 --> 00:39:27.770
and it's non-partisan.

00:39:27.770 --> 00:39:31.310
We need more organizations
like the Union

00:39:31.310 --> 00:39:35.060
of Concerned Scientists
to deal with other issues,

00:39:35.060 --> 00:39:39.950
other public issues, on which
the public and Congress need

00:39:39.950 --> 00:39:41.970
expert advice.

00:39:41.970 --> 00:39:43.970
And I'm just going to
mention two of them, which

00:39:43.970 --> 00:39:48.270
are close to my heart, but I'm
sure you can think of others.

00:39:48.270 --> 00:39:51.080
One of them is space policy.

00:39:51.080 --> 00:39:57.290
Since its founding,
NASA has emphasized

00:39:57.290 --> 00:40:00.500
putting people into
space, rather than

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:07.310
having people or unmanned
vehicles do science in space.

00:40:07.310 --> 00:40:09.910
And this is not a good
way to do science.

00:40:09.910 --> 00:40:12.340
There's not much
that people can do

00:40:12.340 --> 00:40:17.860
in space that can't be done
better and much cheaper

00:40:17.860 --> 00:40:22.120
by unmanned robots
or satellites.

00:40:22.120 --> 00:40:23.350
People radiate heat.

00:40:23.350 --> 00:40:26.530
They bump into things, and
they're very expensive.

00:40:26.530 --> 00:40:28.810
They need food, air, and water.

00:40:28.810 --> 00:40:32.140
And unlike robots,
people want to come back.

00:40:32.140 --> 00:40:33.116
[LAUGHS]

00:40:35.070 --> 00:40:36.530
Which is a real complication.

00:40:39.310 --> 00:40:43.870
Now, NASA has sponsored
great astronomy.

00:40:43.870 --> 00:40:48.910
The things sponsored by NASA are
partly responsible for making

00:40:48.910 --> 00:40:52.120
this-- it's almost a cliché
now to say it's a golden age

00:40:52.120 --> 00:40:56.470
for cosmology, but all
from unmanned vehicles.

00:40:56.470 --> 00:41:00.610
The Hubble Space Telescope, the
Wilkinson Microwave Anisotropy

00:41:00.610 --> 00:41:02.170
Probe, and so on.

00:41:02.170 --> 00:41:04.360
And the area of
planetary physics,

00:41:04.360 --> 00:41:07.105
the unmanned rovers
crawling around Mars.

00:41:09.870 --> 00:41:12.540
Astronomers and
astrophysicists have generally

00:41:12.540 --> 00:41:19.470
been unwilling to criticize NASA
because of its, what I regard,

00:41:19.470 --> 00:41:24.030
as an infantile emphasis
on manned spaceflight.

00:41:24.030 --> 00:41:27.570
Because they have
felt, as, obviously,

00:41:27.570 --> 00:41:31.050
NASA officials feel,
that putting people

00:41:31.050 --> 00:41:33.540
into space appeals
to the public,

00:41:33.540 --> 00:41:37.050
and thereby increases
NASA's budget.

00:41:37.050 --> 00:41:41.190
And some of the money from that
budget goes into real science.

00:41:41.190 --> 00:41:46.300
But this tacit bargain, I
think, is now unraveling.

00:41:46.300 --> 00:41:50.740
The moon/Mars Mission,
announced by President Bush,

00:41:50.740 --> 00:41:52.360
is enormously costly.

00:41:55.320 --> 00:41:57.410
Not only hundreds of
billions of dollars,

00:41:57.410 --> 00:42:01.067
I think close to a
trillion dollars.

00:42:01.067 --> 00:42:03.400
You know, a trillion here, a
trillion there, it adds up.

00:42:03.400 --> 00:42:04.250
[LAUGHS]

00:42:05.950 --> 00:42:12.105
And NASA is, in response,
cutting back on science.

00:42:14.700 --> 00:42:18.570
And you see this in many
areas, from the study

00:42:18.570 --> 00:42:24.600
of climate change, both by
satellites and by observing

00:42:24.600 --> 00:42:27.750
stations here on
earth, and all the way

00:42:27.750 --> 00:42:31.470
to the study of the cosmic
microwave background.

00:42:31.470 --> 00:42:35.760
There are many proposals,
which NASA solicited,

00:42:35.760 --> 00:42:40.440
and which it was going to go
ahead with under the Beyond

00:42:40.440 --> 00:42:44.160
Einstein program, which are
simply going to be deferred,

00:42:44.160 --> 00:42:47.370
or perhaps canceled.

00:42:47.370 --> 00:42:52.590
A good example of this
relates directly to MIT.

00:42:52.590 --> 00:42:59.280
Sam Ting of MIT has developed
a cosmic ray observatory

00:42:59.280 --> 00:43:00.450
called AMS.

00:43:00.450 --> 00:43:04.210
I think that stands for
Advanced Magnetic Spectrograph.

00:43:04.210 --> 00:43:07.530
I'm not sure what
the A stands for.

00:43:07.530 --> 00:43:08.818
What is it?

00:43:08.818 --> 00:43:10.030
AUDIENCE: Alpha.

00:43:10.030 --> 00:43:14.620
Alpha Magnetic Sp-- oh, I would
never have thought of that.

00:43:14.620 --> 00:43:16.870
Over a billion
dollars has already

00:43:16.870 --> 00:43:21.550
been spent on constructing
this, and it is constructed,

00:43:21.550 --> 00:43:25.960
not by NASA, but mostly
by European collaboration.

00:43:25.960 --> 00:43:30.220
NASA had agreed to
take this instrument up

00:43:30.220 --> 00:43:33.130
to the International
Space Station.

00:43:33.130 --> 00:43:37.390
If that were done, it would
be possible to observe

00:43:37.390 --> 00:43:40.990
primary cosmic
rays of high energy

00:43:40.990 --> 00:43:44.680
directly, rather than just
by observing the showers

00:43:44.680 --> 00:43:47.080
that we can see at ground level.

00:43:47.080 --> 00:43:50.620
And it would be the only
significant science ever done

00:43:50.620 --> 00:43:54.400
at the International
Space Station.

00:43:54.400 --> 00:43:57.070
The reason for taking it up to
the International Space Station

00:43:57.070 --> 00:43:59.880
is not that you need
human beings there.

00:43:59.880 --> 00:44:04.130
It is not a counterexample
to what I said earlier.

00:44:04.130 --> 00:44:06.730
It's just that the International
Space Station is there

00:44:06.730 --> 00:44:09.070
above the Earth's atmosphere.

00:44:09.070 --> 00:44:13.690
It's got a lot of solar power,
so you might as well use it

00:44:13.690 --> 00:44:16.720
for something useful for once.

00:44:16.720 --> 00:44:21.580
But NASA has announced,
shortly after the president's

00:44:21.580 --> 00:44:26.110
announcement of his vision,
that the International Space

00:44:26.110 --> 00:44:28.210
Station, from now on,
would be only used

00:44:28.210 --> 00:44:31.330
for purposes furthering
the president's vision

00:44:31.330 --> 00:44:34.270
of the moon and Mars mission.

00:44:34.270 --> 00:44:38.620
And it is, apparently,
unwilling to commit

00:44:38.620 --> 00:44:41.530
what is needed, which is
essentially one quarter of one

00:44:41.530 --> 00:44:46.630
shuttle load, to bring the AMS
up to the International Space

00:44:46.630 --> 00:44:48.250
Station.

00:44:48.250 --> 00:44:50.320
I think this example
shows that it's

00:44:50.320 --> 00:44:56.050
time to form an organization
for space policy

00:44:56.050 --> 00:44:58.180
like the Union of
Concerned Scientists.

00:44:58.180 --> 00:45:01.390
And astronomers and
astrophysicists and physicists

00:45:01.390 --> 00:45:03.880
should begin to speak
up and challenge

00:45:03.880 --> 00:45:08.500
NASA on these childish
judgments it makes.

00:45:08.500 --> 00:45:12.910
The other example is much
further from my own interests--

00:45:12.910 --> 00:45:14.890
and I speak with
some hesitation,

00:45:14.890 --> 00:45:18.760
but I have checked what
I've said with experts.

00:45:18.760 --> 00:45:21.640
It has to do with economics.

00:45:21.640 --> 00:45:25.480
Economics, of course, involves
value judgments, not just

00:45:25.480 --> 00:45:30.140
expertise, and the
value judgments

00:45:30.140 --> 00:45:32.600
are things from the public,
in general, to make.

00:45:32.600 --> 00:45:34.790
But there are some
technical matters

00:45:34.790 --> 00:45:37.340
on which economists
generally agree,

00:45:37.340 --> 00:45:42.200
but which get terribly muddied
in arguments over policy.

00:45:42.200 --> 00:45:43.700
And I will just
give one example.

00:45:46.280 --> 00:45:50.300
There are many things that need
to be done in our country that

00:45:50.300 --> 00:45:53.930
are not being done because
there aren't any funds for them.

00:45:53.930 --> 00:45:55.970
Not because we can't
think of how to do them,

00:45:55.970 --> 00:45:58.530
but because there just
isn't money for them.

00:45:58.530 --> 00:46:03.020
One is to inspect and
fix bridges and tunnels

00:46:03.020 --> 00:46:08.810
throughout the country,
inspect imports for everything

00:46:08.810 --> 00:46:12.500
from nuclear weapons
in freight containers

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:17.930
to lead paint on toys,
monitor prescription drugs

00:46:17.930 --> 00:46:22.790
after the FDA has approved them
in the years following when

00:46:22.790 --> 00:46:27.020
they go on being used, Break
the logjam in the United States

00:46:27.020 --> 00:46:32.660
Patent Office, Provide drug
rehabilitation for drug

00:46:32.660 --> 00:46:34.520
addicts who ask for it.

00:46:34.520 --> 00:46:39.620
Just amazing to me that we have
this enormous criminal justice

00:46:39.620 --> 00:46:42.290
system which
punishes drug addicts

00:46:42.290 --> 00:46:44.990
and we don't provide
rehabilitation

00:46:44.990 --> 00:46:48.110
for people who want to be
cured of their addiction.

00:46:48.110 --> 00:46:51.950
Have something like
the World War II GI

00:46:51.950 --> 00:46:58.010
Bill, which provides
educational benefits of the sort

00:46:58.010 --> 00:47:02.180
awarded after World War
II to all our veterans,

00:47:02.180 --> 00:47:05.270
both so that people would
be more willing to serve,

00:47:05.270 --> 00:47:07.940
and also so that
after their service,

00:47:07.940 --> 00:47:10.070
we will have a more
educated workforce.

00:47:13.070 --> 00:47:19.220
Sometimes it's hard to argue
for scientific projects which

00:47:19.220 --> 00:47:22.430
cost millions, or tens,
or hundreds of millions

00:47:22.430 --> 00:47:27.020
of dollars, when there were all
these other needs which are not

00:47:27.020 --> 00:47:29.720
being funded.

00:47:29.720 --> 00:47:33.860
We need all of those, including
the scientific projects.

00:47:33.860 --> 00:47:38.900
Many people will agree with
most of the items on this list,

00:47:38.900 --> 00:47:41.600
but it's often said that
although these things are

00:47:41.600 --> 00:47:46.250
worthwhile, we can't
do them because we

00:47:46.250 --> 00:47:51.260
need to cut taxes in order
to stimulate the economy.

00:47:51.260 --> 00:47:55.390
Or we can't raise taxes, because
that would hurt the economy,

00:47:55.390 --> 00:47:58.340
and the economy is
the thing which allows

00:47:58.340 --> 00:48:00.320
everything else to be done.

00:48:00.320 --> 00:48:03.170
Now, I've spoken to a
number of economists,

00:48:03.170 --> 00:48:06.740
including one
distinguished one from MIT,

00:48:06.740 --> 00:48:10.780
and I'm told that
this is a fallacy,

00:48:10.780 --> 00:48:13.070
and it's sort of
obviously a fallacy.

00:48:13.070 --> 00:48:18.620
In the short run, a dollar
that is spent by government

00:48:18.620 --> 00:48:22.190
has precisely the same
effect on the economy

00:48:22.190 --> 00:48:25.100
as a dollar which
is freed by tax cuts

00:48:25.100 --> 00:48:28.250
to be spent in the
private economy.

00:48:28.250 --> 00:48:29.780
The only difference
is whether you

00:48:29.780 --> 00:48:34.260
get public goods or private
goods for the dollar.

00:48:34.260 --> 00:48:39.530
But as far as the effect on
the economy, it's the same.

00:48:39.530 --> 00:48:43.310
I'm comparing a dollar tax
cut with a dollar spent

00:48:43.310 --> 00:48:46.310
by government because they
have identical effects

00:48:46.310 --> 00:48:47.840
on the deficit.

00:48:47.840 --> 00:48:50.270
And I'm claiming,
and I'm told, they

00:48:50.270 --> 00:48:54.080
have identical effects
on, say, employment,

00:48:54.080 --> 00:48:58.570
or other indices of the economy.

00:48:58.570 --> 00:49:01.630
Actually, I was told that a
dollar spent by government

00:49:01.630 --> 00:49:05.920
is slightly more effective
because a dollar tax

00:49:05.920 --> 00:49:08.690
cut, some fraction of
it, would be saved.

00:49:08.690 --> 00:49:10.240
But the difference
is very small.

00:49:12.790 --> 00:49:15.280
Now, of course, the choice
between public goods

00:49:15.280 --> 00:49:18.900
and private goods is
a choice for voters.

00:49:18.900 --> 00:49:23.500
It's not a choice for
professional economists,

00:49:23.500 --> 00:49:26.320
much less for physicists.

00:49:26.320 --> 00:49:34.570
But it is a choice that should
be made without the illusion

00:49:34.570 --> 00:49:37.840
that there is something about
the private economy which

00:49:37.840 --> 00:49:44.720
is uniquely healthy in a way
that the public sector is not.

00:49:44.720 --> 00:49:49.390
And I think it is
time, on such issues,

00:49:49.390 --> 00:49:53.770
for the public and Congress
to get better advice

00:49:53.770 --> 00:49:56.110
from the economic profession.

00:49:56.110 --> 00:50:01.550
Perhaps it's time for a Union
of Concerned Economists.

00:50:01.550 --> 00:50:07.160
Well, as I said, it is
wonderful to be back here,

00:50:07.160 --> 00:50:13.520
but for me, it's also
sad, because so many

00:50:13.520 --> 00:50:18.530
of the people that I knew
here at MIT in the late '60s

00:50:18.530 --> 00:50:22.040
and early '70s are now gone.

00:50:22.040 --> 00:50:24.620
Certainly too many
to list, but I

00:50:24.620 --> 00:50:29.300
want to mention three
who were important to me

00:50:29.300 --> 00:50:35.000
and for whom, posthumously,
I'd like to say thanks.

00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:41.790
One is Dave Frisch, who, when
visiting Copenhagen, where

00:50:41.790 --> 00:50:43.800
I was a first-year
graduate student,

00:50:43.800 --> 00:50:47.670
gave me my first
research problem.

00:50:47.670 --> 00:50:53.870
Another is Viki Weisskopf,
who brought me to MIT.

00:50:53.870 --> 00:50:57.260
And the third is
Francis Lowe, who

00:50:57.260 --> 00:51:01.220
was a never-ending
source of wisdom

00:51:01.220 --> 00:51:05.040
on physics and much else.

00:51:05.040 --> 00:51:10.410
Francis and Natalie
were enormously friendly

00:51:10.410 --> 00:51:16.750
to my wife Louise and
to me, and we love them.

00:51:16.750 --> 00:51:21.480
I was very sorry
that I was in Europe

00:51:21.480 --> 00:51:26.850
at the time of the
memorial for Francis Lowe,

00:51:26.850 --> 00:51:30.840
and one of the reasons I was
glad to be able to come here

00:51:30.840 --> 00:51:36.390
today is that it would give me
a chance to make up for that

00:51:36.390 --> 00:51:43.770
and to say how much I miss him
and Natalie, as well as Vicki

00:51:43.770 --> 00:51:45.990
and Dave.

00:51:45.990 --> 00:51:51.180
But life goes on,
and I see there

00:51:51.180 --> 00:51:55.740
is a new generation of
brilliant theorists here at MIT.

00:51:55.740 --> 00:51:59.130
I had dinner last night
with a number of them

00:51:59.130 --> 00:52:01.740
and thoroughly enjoyed that.

00:52:01.740 --> 00:52:05.490
And now, they have a
wonderful new center

00:52:05.490 --> 00:52:07.710
in which to do their work.

00:52:07.710 --> 00:52:16.130
And I congratulate them for the
opportunity of working here.

00:52:16.130 --> 00:52:21.980
And thank you and wish you,
for lunch, bon appétit.

00:52:21.980 --> 00:52:23.940
[APPLAUSE]

00:52:35.220 --> 00:52:38.460
PRESENTER: We do have time
for questions or comments,

00:52:38.460 --> 00:52:43.648
if anyone wishes
to say something.

00:52:43.648 --> 00:52:48.800
AUDIENCE: I have a
question for Bob Laughlin.

00:52:48.800 --> 00:52:53.120
Bob, you kept talking about
physics with a small p.

00:52:53.120 --> 00:52:57.230
And I was wondering, what
is physics with a large p?

00:52:57.230 --> 00:52:58.400
What is the distinction?

00:53:02.360 --> 00:53:09.230
LAUGHLIN: Roman, that's
just jargon that I thought

00:53:09.230 --> 00:53:10.850
everybody knew what I meant.

00:53:10.850 --> 00:53:16.130
Just in case it didn't, physics
with a big P at Stanford

00:53:16.130 --> 00:53:20.420
has to do with
departmental warfare, OK?

00:53:20.420 --> 00:53:21.274
[LAUGHS]

00:53:21.274 --> 00:53:22.970
AUDIENCE: See, we
don't have that

00:53:22.970 --> 00:53:24.200
here so we didn't understand.

00:53:24.200 --> 00:53:25.117
LAUGHLIN: Yeah, right.

00:53:25.117 --> 00:53:26.158
[LAUGHS]

00:53:26.990 --> 00:53:28.160
Let me tell you--

00:53:28.160 --> 00:53:30.680
since I've got the microphone,
I get to tell you a joke now.

00:53:30.680 --> 00:53:34.910
One of my best students
ever is John Bakalson Soffi,

00:53:34.910 --> 00:53:38.420
who's now in the area now
running a biotech company.

00:53:38.420 --> 00:53:40.830
And he said, you know, I've
got Stanford figured out.

00:53:40.830 --> 00:53:43.790
He said, at Stanford, we
have two physics departments.

00:53:43.790 --> 00:53:45.980
We have the Department
of Physics--

00:53:45.980 --> 00:53:48.290
sorry, we have the
Department of Applied Physics

00:53:48.290 --> 00:53:50.644
and we have the Department
of Unapplied Physics.

00:53:50.644 --> 00:53:51.144
[LAUGHS]

00:53:51.144 --> 00:53:52.410
OK.

00:53:52.410 --> 00:53:57.882
Now that's what I mean by
physics with a large P.

00:53:57.882 --> 00:54:00.360
PRESENTER: I can add to this,
that a long time ago, when

00:54:00.360 --> 00:54:02.670
I was thinking about
taking a job at Princeton,

00:54:02.670 --> 00:54:05.310
Murph Goldberger described
Princeton physics to me

00:54:05.310 --> 00:54:08.620
by saying, at Princeton, we
do physics with a capital F.

00:54:08.620 --> 00:54:11.070
[LAUGHS]

00:54:17.920 --> 00:54:18.420
Max.

00:54:23.840 --> 00:54:26.090
AUDIENCE: I have another
question for Bob Laughlin.

00:54:26.090 --> 00:54:32.060
We've heard a number of slightly
gloomy and cautionary remarks

00:54:32.060 --> 00:54:34.010
throughout the morning here.

00:54:34.010 --> 00:54:36.290
And I was wondering, on
the topic of knowledge

00:54:36.290 --> 00:54:39.380
sequestration, whether you
feel that there is also

00:54:39.380 --> 00:54:43.880
a glimmer of hope
coming from the internet

00:54:43.880 --> 00:54:45.560
and the globalization
of information,

00:54:45.560 --> 00:54:47.060
where it becomes
harder and harder,

00:54:47.060 --> 00:54:49.880
even for an entire
nation, to stifle

00:54:49.880 --> 00:54:54.810
information that can become
accessible from somewhere else?

00:54:54.810 --> 00:54:57.290
LAUGHLIN: This is not the time
to have a discussion on that

00:54:57.290 --> 00:54:59.510
because we're talking
about the Green Center,

00:54:59.510 --> 00:55:03.990
but since you asked the
question, just very tersely,

00:55:03.990 --> 00:55:04.670
no.

00:55:04.670 --> 00:55:07.500
The internet has an
economics problem,

00:55:07.500 --> 00:55:11.690
and the problem is that
the communication is free.

00:55:11.690 --> 00:55:13.490
And you notice what's
happening with it,

00:55:13.490 --> 00:55:16.580
it's filling up with garbage.

00:55:16.580 --> 00:55:20.750
And what is the end point of
that, I'm reasonably sure,

00:55:20.750 --> 00:55:23.240
is another version
of television,

00:55:23.240 --> 00:55:25.220
in which there's
tons of information

00:55:25.220 --> 00:55:28.430
on the internet,
but no knowledge.

00:55:28.430 --> 00:55:33.170
Actually, when it comes
to industrial things,

00:55:33.170 --> 00:55:35.757
lawyers have teams of people
preventing the knowledge

00:55:35.757 --> 00:55:37.340
from being accessible
on the internet.

00:55:37.340 --> 00:55:40.130
If you put it on the
internet, they'll sue you.

00:55:40.130 --> 00:55:42.260
And of course, there's
disinformation as well.

00:55:44.900 --> 00:55:48.560
So no, I don't think the
internet is the revolution

00:55:48.560 --> 00:55:50.580
that everybody thought it was.

00:55:50.580 --> 00:55:57.140
And then I reiterate that in
this rapidly changing time,

00:55:57.140 --> 00:56:00.680
you can't evade the
responsibility, as a physicist,

00:56:00.680 --> 00:56:03.170
to be the Renaissance
person that the engineering

00:56:03.170 --> 00:56:04.430
university needs.

00:56:04.430 --> 00:56:06.280
It's very, very important.

00:56:13.330 --> 00:56:16.920
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] open
source for space probe.

00:56:16.920 --> 00:56:20.482
There's another project that's
going on in the Department

00:56:20.482 --> 00:56:21.190
department of Ed.

00:56:21.190 --> 00:56:26.710
You know, you and I witnessed
the transformation from the AC

00:56:26.710 --> 00:56:28.510
gives you permission
to be unique,

00:56:28.510 --> 00:56:31.620
and that's united organization.

00:56:31.620 --> 00:56:37.200
In other related administration,
that we've been [INAUDIBLE]..

00:56:37.200 --> 00:56:39.542
There would be a
$15 billion wealth

00:56:39.542 --> 00:56:42.580
for which there is no
scientific reason, really.

00:56:42.580 --> 00:56:47.110
And this is very similar
to the space station.

00:56:47.110 --> 00:56:51.040
PRESENTER: Steve, you
want to say something?

00:56:51.040 --> 00:56:53.490
Let me just add to
that, Bruno, that one

00:56:53.490 --> 00:56:55.990
of the most enlightened things
that the Department of Energy

00:56:55.990 --> 00:56:58.480
has done for the last
60 years is continuously

00:56:58.480 --> 00:57:02.464
support theoretical
fundamental physics at MIT.

00:57:02.464 --> 00:57:02.964
[LAUGHS]

00:57:02.964 --> 00:57:03.952
[APPLAUSE]

00:57:09.150 --> 00:57:13.000
LAUGHLIN: Look, I
have mixed feelings

00:57:13.000 --> 00:57:15.250
about the Department of
Energy, like everybody else,

00:57:15.250 --> 00:57:18.420
but let me make a
prediction since I'm going

00:57:18.420 --> 00:57:20.570
to put on my Livermore hat now.

00:57:20.570 --> 00:57:23.380
Of all the many faults
that Livermore has,

00:57:23.380 --> 00:57:26.930
the big laser is
not one of them.

00:57:26.930 --> 00:57:30.220
I think what's going to
happen soon is that they're

00:57:30.220 --> 00:57:33.350
going to make a pellet go off.

00:57:33.350 --> 00:57:37.820
When that happens, it
will change the world.

00:57:37.820 --> 00:57:40.430
There will be banner headlines
in the New York Times,

00:57:40.430 --> 00:57:44.180
and suddenly, people's
thinking about the physics

00:57:44.180 --> 00:57:47.230
and its relevance
to the energy future

00:57:47.230 --> 00:57:51.110
will have a 180 degree shift.

00:57:51.110 --> 00:57:54.390
Now, of course, I'm a theorist,
so I make concrete predictions.

00:57:54.390 --> 00:57:56.210
So either that
happens or it doesn't.

00:57:56.210 --> 00:57:57.628
I'm willing to take bets.

00:57:57.628 --> 00:57:59.002
[LAUGHS]

00:58:02.364 --> 00:58:04.624
AUDIENCE: I think we
can discuss privately.

00:58:04.624 --> 00:58:05.166
LAUGHLIN: OK.

00:58:05.166 --> 00:58:06.100
[LAUGHS]

00:58:06.505 --> 00:58:08.130
PRESENTER: Other
questions or comments?

00:58:11.980 --> 00:58:14.740
Well, if not, let me thank
everybody, the organizers

00:58:14.740 --> 00:58:15.820
and the speakers.

00:58:15.820 --> 00:58:17.820
And everyone enjoy
lunch, and there's

00:58:17.820 --> 00:58:21.430
a dedication ceremony
at 2:30 in the atrium

00:58:21.430 --> 00:58:23.720
on top of the Sol Lewitt Art.

00:58:23.720 --> 00:58:24.540
Thank you.

00:58:24.540 --> 00:58:27.590
[APPLAUSE]

